    {
        "user": "U036YAYMM",
        "type": "message",
        "ts": "1645808345.899359",
        "client_msg_id": "b5356b67-817c-41bd-aed2-427f478bd6d1",
        "text": "A shout-out for Noah’s tenant organizing coverage yesterday in Next City’s newsletter: <https:\/\/nextcity.org\/urbanist-news\/housing-in-brief-nyc-mayor-increases-policing-of-homelessness-on-the-subway>\n\nSan Francisco Tenants Are Close to Having Collective Bargaining Rights\nTenants in San Francisco could soon be permitted to form collective bargaining entities — much like labor unions — to negotiate debt, rent increases and building maintenance with landlords. The Board of Supervisors voted on Feb. 15 to approve<https:\/\/sfgov.legistar.com\/LegislationDetail.aspx?ID=5191199&amp;GUID=F81B312D-FBF7-4122-BE1C-0313544C15C8| the measure>, according to the <https:\/\/www.sfpublicpress.org\/sf-tenants-to-gain-new-powers-in-negotiations-with-landlords\/|San Francisco Public Press>. The board must vote _yes_ a second time for the bill to become law. Landlords would be required to negotiate “in good faith” with tenant associations. The bill prohibits landlords from interfering with tenant organizing, whether by residents of the building or from visitors. The protections would cover private buildings with five or more units, and 50% of a building’s units would need to sign on for a newly formed tenant association to be recognized. It would be the first city legislation in the country granting collective bargaining rights to tenants in privately owned residential buildings. Residents of federal public housing have had the right to organize <https:\/\/d3n8a8pro7vhmx.cloudfront.net\/saveourhomesusa\/pages\/241\/attachments\/original\/1508355249\/F_Federal_Register_HUD_30_copies_2-2_stapled.pdf?1508355249|since 2002>.",
        "team": "T036YAYMK",
        "user_team": "T036YAYMK",
        "source_team": "T036YAYMK",
        "user_profile": {
            "avatar_hash": "a8915bfb0098",
            "image_72": "https:\/\/avatars.slack-edge.com\/2018-02-01\/308107995713_a8915bfb00981894f216_72.png",
            "first_name": "mstoll",
            "real_name": "mstoll",
            "display_name": "Michael Stoll",
            "team": "T036YAYMK",
            "name": "mstoll",
            "is_restricted": false,
            "is_ultra_restricted": false
        },
        "attachments": [
            {
                "from_url": "https:\/\/nextcity.org\/urbanist-news\/housing-in-brief-nyc-mayor-increases-policing-of-homelessness-on-the-subway",
                "image_url": "https:\/\/nextcity.org\/images\/daily\/_resized\/51888496442_6b8e08e1b7_k.jpg",
                "image_width": 339,
                "image_height": 250,
                "image_bytes": 146369,
                "service_icon": "https:\/\/nextcity.org\/favicon.ico",
                "id": 1,
                "original_url": "https:\/\/nextcity.org\/urbanist-news\/housing-in-brief-nyc-mayor-increases-policing-of-homelessness-on-the-subway",
                "fallback": "Housing in Brief: NYC Mayor Increases Policing of Homelessness on the Subway",
                "text": "Also: S.F. tenants are close to collective bargaining rights.",
                "title": "Housing in Brief: NYC Mayor Increases Policing of Homelessness on the Subway",
                "title_link": "https:\/\/nextcity.org\/urbanist-news\/housing-in-brief-nyc-mayor-increases-policing-of-homelessness-on-the-subway",
                "service_name": "nextcity.org"
            }
        ],
        "blocks": [
            {
                "type": "rich_text",
                "block_id": "LeITB",
                "elements": [
                    {
                        "type": "rich_text_section",
                        "elements": [
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": "A shout-out for Noah’s tenant organizing coverage yesterday in Next City’s newsletter: "
                            },
                            {
                                "type": "link",
                                "url": "https:\/\/nextcity.org\/urbanist-news\/housing-in-brief-nyc-mayor-increases-policing-of-homelessness-on-the-subway"
                            },
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": "\n\nSan Francisco Tenants Are Close to Having Collective Bargaining Rights\nTenants in San Francisco could soon be permitted to form collective bargaining entities — much like labor unions — to negotiate debt, rent increases and building maintenance with landlords. The Board of Supervisors voted on Feb. 15 to approve"
                            },
                            {
                                "type": "link",
                                "url": "https:\/\/sfgov.legistar.com\/LegislationDetail.aspx?ID=5191199&GUID=F81B312D-FBF7-4122-BE1C-0313544C15C8",
                                "text": " the measure"
                            },
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": ", according to the "
                            },
                            {
                                "type": "link",
                                "url": "https:\/\/www.sfpublicpress.org\/sf-tenants-to-gain-new-powers-in-negotiations-with-landlords\/",
                                "text": "San Francisco Public Press"
                            },
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": ". The board must vote "
                            },
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": "yes",
                                "style": {
                                    "italic": true
                                }
                            },
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": " a second time for the bill to become law. Landlords would be required to negotiate “in good faith” with tenant associations. The bill prohibits landlords from interfering with tenant organizing, whether by residents of the building or from visitors. The protections would cover private buildings with five or more units, and 50% of a building’s units would need to sign on for a newly formed tenant association to be recognized. It would be the first city legislation in the country granting collective bargaining rights to tenants in privately owned residential buildings. Residents of federal public housing have had the right to organize "
                            },
                            {
                                "type": "link",
                                "url": "https:\/\/d3n8a8pro7vhmx.cloudfront.net\/saveourhomesusa\/pages\/241\/attachments\/original\/1508355249\/F_Federal_Register_HUD_30_copies_2-2_stapled.pdf?1508355249",
                                "text": "since 2002"
                            },
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": "."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ]
            }
        ],
